# November 22, 2026: the Greene County Mountaintop (Haines Falls, Tannersville, Hunter, Lexington, Windham, Ashland,
# Prattsville) and Pine Hill just over the line in Ulster County. Drawn at random by tools/choose.py --state ny
# (region cat), which offered Long Eddy, Onteora Park and Tannersville; the Atlas had only the Haines Falls hotel sites,
# two tanneries and the Durham bridges in this country, and nothing in Tannersville, Hunter village, Windham, Ashland,
# Lexington, Prattsville village or Pine Hill. Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py,
# including the Greene County Register list), HMdb.org markers through tools/page.py, the Mountain Top Historical
# Society's pages, the Zadock Pratt Museum's own page, NYSDEC's Hunter-West Kill Wilderness and Kaaterskill Wild Forest
# pages, Catskill Mountaineer (Escarpment Trail), the Episcopal Diocese of Albany and Hudson Valley One. NPGallery gave
# only placeholders for every Greene County form tried.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HM(n): return L('HMdb marker '+str(n),'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+str(n))

BATCH={'key':'cat_zxtw','label':'Greene County Mountaintop: Tannersville, Hunter, Prattsville, and Pine Hill','added':'2026-11-22 05:49'}

DEC_HWK=L('NYSDEC: Hunter-West Kill Wilderness','https://dec.ny.gov/places/hunter-west-kill-wilderness')
DEC_KWF=L('NYSDEC: Kaaterskill Wild Forest','https://dec.ny.gov/places/kaaterskill-wild-forest')
MTHS=L('Mountain Top Historical Society: visit us','https://legacy.mths.org/visit-us.html')

PLACES=[
N('haines-falls-station','Haines Falls railroad station (Mountain Top Historical Society)','Haines Falls','cat','rail','standing',42.19588,-74.09104,'exact','open','walk','stations','1913–1940',
  'A wooden depot of the Ulster and Delaware Railroad\'s Kaaterskill Branch, which ran trains to the hotels of the Mountaintop from 1883 until January 1940. The station began as a stop of the narrow-gauge Kaaterskill Railroad, called Haines Corners, and was one of the busiest on the line. The present building dates from 1913; the historical society calls it the last of its kind in Greene County. It was listed on the National Register in 1996 and is the society\'s headquarters.',
  'The depot on the society\'s campus off Route 23A, beside the Kaaterskill Rail Trail on the old branch bed; the trail goes on about a mile and a half to the top of Kaaterskill Falls and North-South Lake. The society says the station is open during its public programs and events, seasonally in summer and fall, and by request. Interior not checked.',
  'The last Ulster and Delaware depot on the Mountaintop.',
  'Mountain Top Historical Society campus, 5132 Route 23A; the campus and the rail trail are open every day of the year. Park at the visitors\' center lot and follow the paved road past the outbuilding.',
  links=[W('Haines Falls station'),MTHS,L('Mountain Top Historical Society','https://mths.org'),HM(187709),HM(147049)],
  th=[42.19473,-74.09247],thn='Mountain Top Historical Society visitors\' center lot',
  brick='no',hours='Campus and rail trail every day; station open for programs and events, seasonally in summer and fall, and by request'),
N('hunter-mountain-fire-tower','Hunter Mountain Fire Tower','Hunter','cat','landmark','standing',42.17793,-74.22977,'exact','public','hike','','1909; steel tower 1917',
  'The first of 23 fire lookout towers the state built in the Catskills: a wooden tower went up on Hunter Mountain in 1909, and eight years later the Conservation Commission replaced it with the present 60-foot steel tower, hauled in sections up the road from Spruceton by horses. In 1953 the tower and its observer\'s cabin were moved about 1,000 feet to the true summit. Left unused, it was saved from removal in the 1990s, listed on the National Register in 1997, and restored with the cabin by local volunteers and DEC.',
  'The steel tower on the summit with the observer\'s cabin just to the southeast, its windows meshed against porcupines. Wikipedia says the top cab is locked except when volunteers staff it on some weekends. Footings of the first tower are still visible at the trail junction where it stood before 1953.',
  'The highest fire tower still standing in New York.',
  'State Forest Preserve land (the tower stands in DEC\'s Rusk Mountain Wild Forest), open for recreation all year. The usual route is the blue Spruceton Trail, the old truck road, from the trailhead lot on Spruceton Road (County Route 6), a climb of more than 2,000 feet; the Becker Hollow Trail from Route 214 and the Hunter Mountain Trail from the Devil\'s Path also reach it.',
  links=[W('Hunter Mountain Fire Tower'),DEC_HWK],
  th=[42.1847,-74.27189],thn='Spruceton Trail parking, Spruceton Road',brick='no'),
N('laymans-monument','Layman\'s Monument','Haines Falls','cat','curio','standing',42.18902,-74.06819,'exact','public','hike','','1900',
  'A stone monument on the Escarpment Trail to Frank D. Layman, a firefighter who died fighting a forest fire on the cliffs above Kaaterskill Clove. The Register list gives the fire as 1901; the hiking site Catskill Mountaineer says he died on August 10, 1900, aged 25, and that the monument stands where his body was found. Listed on the National Register in 2003.',
  'The monument beside the blue-marked Escarpment Trail below Kaaterskill High Peak\'s eastern ledges, reached by a steep, rocky descent and climb (the hiking site\'s mileage chart puts it at 1,836 feet elevation). Not checked on the ground.',
  'A firefighter\'s memorial on a cliff trail.',
  'Kaaterskill Wild Forest, state Forest Preserve, open all year. DEC sends hikers to the Scutt Road, Laurel House Road or South Lake parking areas; parking is banned in the Route 23A pull-offs in Kaaterskill Clove from May through October, and illegally parked cars are towed. From Laurel House Road follow the blue Escarpment Trail south past the Kaaterskill Falls overlook.',
  links=[DEC_KWF,L('Catskill Mountaineer: Southern Escarpment Trail','https://catskillmountaineer.com/NSL-escarpment-southern.html'),
         L('Register list for Greene County (Wikipedia)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Register_of_Historic_Places_listings_in_Greene_County,_New_York')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.19579,-74.06333],thn='Laurel House Road Parking Area',brick='?'),
N('kiersted-tannery-east-hunter','Kiersted tannery site (East Hunter)','Haines Falls','cat','industry','traces',42.18542,-74.07008,'area','public','walk','','',
  'A hemlock-bark tannery at the foot of Buttermilk Falls in Kaaterskill Clove, the centre of a settlement called East Hunter that had its own school and post office. The Mountain Top Historical Society\'s marker on Route 23A says a few foundations of the tannery remain. No dates were found.',
  'The society\'s marker at the Route 23A pull-off, and the foundations it mentions near the foot of Buttermilk Falls; where exactly they lie was not found, and the pin marks the marker. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A tannery village that left a few foundations in the clove.',
  'The pull-off and the land round it are mapped as Kaaterskill Wild Forest (state Forest Preserve). Parking in the 23A pull-offs is banned from May 1 through October 15; park at DEC\'s Laurel House Road lot then, and mind the traffic on the narrow highway.',
  links=[HM(147059),DEC_KWF],th=[42.19579,-74.06333],thn='Laurel House Road Parking Area',brick='?'),
N('all-souls-church-tannersville','All Souls Church, Onteora','Tannersville','cat','worship','standing',42.22458,-74.1339,'exact','view','drive','','1894',
  'A small stone Episcopal chapel in the Gothic Revival style, built in 1894 at Onteora Park, the summer colony north of Tannersville. It was enlarged in the 1910s with a bigger chancel and transepts. Listed on the National Register in 1993.',
  'The stone church with its big square bell tower at the southwest corner, which is also the main entrance, on County Road 25. Services and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from County Road 25 (1107 County Road 25, Onteora Park). Onteora Park round it is a private summer colony.',
  links=[W('All Souls Church (Tannersville, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('st-john-evangelist-tannersville','Church of St. John the Evangelist','Tannersville','cat','worship','standing',42.18222,-74.14806,'site','view','drive','','1885',
  'An Episcopal summer church opened in August 1885, designed by William Halsey Wood. Before it, the Cowley fathers (the Society of St. John the Evangelist) held services in hotels such as the Catskill Mountain House and the Laurel House. Alexander and Mrs. Hemsley, owners of the Blythewood resort, gave the land and built the road to it, now Philadelphia Hill Road; Wood married their daughter Florence here in 1889. Listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'A one-story frame church on partial fieldstone walls, with a steep gable roof and a large square belfry on the ridge; Wikipedia describes a vaulted wooden interior with a clerestory. Services and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'On Philadelphia Hill Road south of the village; look from the road. Whether and when it opens was not checked.',
  links=[W('Church of St. John the Evangelist (Hunter, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('tannersville-main-street','Tannersville Main Street: the painted village','Tannersville','cat','town','standing',42.19574,-74.12769,'site','public','walk','','1820s–',
  'A tannery settlement from shortly after the Revolution (the tanneries opened about 1820) that became a busy summer resort when the railroad arrived in 1882. The village was incorporated in 1895. Its Main Street was listed on the National Register as a historic district in 2008. The Hunter Foundation\'s Paint Program has painted the downtown buildings in bright pastels, often with cartoon pictures on the shutters, and the marker calls it "The Painted Village in the Sky".',
  'Walk Main Street (Route 23A) between the two Mountain Top Historical Society markers and the Pomeroy "Rip Van Winkle" marker at County Route 25. Which buildings are old was not checked building by building.',
  'A tannery town painted in candy colours.',
  'Village street, free to walk; the buildings are private shops and houses. Park on the street.',
  links=[W('Tannersville, New York'),HM(147102),HM(147113),HM(147099)],brick='?'),
N('hunter-synagogue','Hunter Synagogue','Hunter','cat','worship','standing',42.21073,-74.21557,'exact','view','drive','','1909–1914',
  'A wooden synagogue built between 1909 and 1914 on Main Street in Hunter, a Queen Anne-inspired building of one and a half stories. Listed on the National Register in 1999, with a shed of about 1910 on the lot.',
  'The frame synagogue at 7879 Main Street. A few metres away is the Odd Fellows hall (Lockwood Lodge No. 653), also on the Register; Wikipedia gives it 1917 in its text and 1913 in its categories. Services and interior not checked; the congregation\'s own site could not be opened when this was written.',
  '',
  'Seen from Main Street (Route 23A), Hunter. Park on the street.',
  links=[W('Hunter Synagogue'),W('IOOF Hall (Hunter, New York)'),HM(159030),HM(159036)],brick='no'),
N('zadock-pratt-museum','Zadock Pratt House (Zadock Pratt Museum)','Prattsville','cat','landmark','standing',42.31471,-74.43235,'exact','open','drive','','1828',
  'The house of Zadock Pratt (1790–1871), tanner, banker and congressman, who planned the village of Prattsville and built what the museum\'s marker calls the world\'s largest tannery. Built in 1828 as a two-story Federal house of massive hemlock posts and beams, it was dressed up about 1856 with broad eaves and a two-story portico on octagonal columns. A house museum since 1959, it lost much of its ground floor to Hurricane Irene in 2011. Listed on the National Register in 1986.',
  'The house on Main Street with its portico, the original well, and two ginkgo trees Pratt planted about 1825. The museum shows the hemlock-tanning era of the northern Catskills and Pratt\'s life. Pratt Rock, his carved cliff, is about 2 km east on Route 23.',
  'The planned tannery town\'s founder at home.',
  'Zadock Pratt Museum, 14540 Main Street (Route 23), supported by the Town of Prattsville. Hours from the museum\'s own home page; an older Hudson River Valley Heritage Area sheet gave Thursday to Sunday afternoons.',
  links=[W('Zadock Pratt House'),L('Zadock Pratt Museum','https://zadockprattmuseum.org/'),HM(132716)],
  brick='no',hours='May to October, Saturdays and Sundays 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('laraway-inn-prattsville','John and Martinus Laraway Inn','Prattsville','cat','hotel','standing',42.31861,-74.43542,'exact','view','drive','','1785',
  'A stagecoach inn built by the brothers John and Martinus Laraway in 1785,. It was the Fowler House Hotel in the late 1800s and has been the O\'Hara family\'s home since 1919. It came through the Hurricane Irene flood of 2011 and was listed on the National Register in 2012.',
  'The old inn at the west end of Main Street, with its 2012 marker: a white clapboard house with a two-story columned porch and a red brick chimney in a 2015 photograph. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1785 stage stop that outlasted Irene.',
  'A private home; look from Main Street (Route 23).',
  links=[HM(234508)],brick='some'),
N('prattsville-reformed-church','Prattsville Reformed Dutch Church','Prattsville','cat','worship','standing',42.31702,-74.43532,'exact','view','drive','','1804; rebuilt 1835',
  'The congregation began in 1798 as the Society of Schoharie Kill, the first church in the mountain towns of Greene County, and built a meeting house here on the western curve of Main Street in 1804; it was rebuilt in 1835 in the Greek Revival and Federal styles, and restored in 1971–1972. Hurricane Irene badly damaged it in 2011, and Wikipedia says the congregation worships in another building until it is repaired. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'The church at 14630 Main Street and its 1932 state marker. Whether the repairs are done, and the interior, were not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Main Street (Route 23). Service times not checked.',
  links=[W('Prattsville Reformed Dutch Church'),HM(62449)],brick='?'),
N('trinity-episcopal-ashland','Trinity Episcopal Church, Ashland','Ashland','cat','worship','standing',42.29864,-74.29772,'site','view','drive','','1879',
  'A board-and-batten Carpenter Gothic church built in 1879 for a parish incorporated on May 20, 1799, the earliest Episcopal congregation in the old Town of Windham. It has a three-story entry and bell tower. Listed on the National Register in 1996. A 1932 state marker nearby marks where the first Congregational church of Ashland was built in 1799.',
  'The wooden church on Route 23 southwest of County Route 19, with its 1932 marker (reported damaged). Services and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Route 23. Service times not checked.',
  links=[W('Trinity Episcopal Church (Ashland, New York)'),HM(269146),HM(269145)],brick='no'),
N('mitchell-hollow-union-chapel','Mitchell Hollow Union Chapel','Windham','cat','worship','standing',42.35306,-74.24528,'site','view','drive','','1897',
  'A small chapel built in 1897 by the local Methodist Episcopal and Presbyterian congregations together, in Mitchell Hollow north of Windham. It is a one-story clapboard building on a stone and concrete foundation, with a full pedimented portico and a two-stage bell tower. Listed on the National Register in 2001.',
  'The white chapel with its portico and tower on Mill Road. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from Mill Road, Windham.',
  links=[W('Union Chapel (Windham, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('lexington-house','Lexington House','Lexington','cat','hotel','standing',42.23972,-74.36667,'site','view','drive','','1883',
  'A middle-class summer hotel built in 1883 for John P. Van Valkenburg on the south side of Schoharie Creek, designed by the local architect Jerome Campbell, with Italianate and Eastlake details, thirty rooms and a broad verandah with balconies above. It became Camp Lexington, a theatre camp for children, in 1931, and from 1976 the home of the Lexington Conservatory Theatre. The grounds keep the Morse Inn (about 1881), an ice house, a wagon house and a shed; the River Theater of about 1887 stood until about 2011, when it was badly damaged. Listed on the National Register in 1986.',
  'The hotel with its verandah and upper balconies across the creek from the hamlet. Present use and interior not checked.',
  'A railroad-era resort that became a theatre.',
  'Private property; look from the road. Not open to visitors as far as was found.',
  links=[W('Lexington House')],brick='?'),
N('pine-hill-hamlet','Pine Hill hamlet','Pine Hill','cat','town','standing',42.13306,-74.48028,'site','public','walk','hotels','c. 1800–1962',
  'A mountain hamlet below Belleayre, listed on the National Register in 2012 as a district of 125 buildings that grew between about 1800 and 1962, with houses from Greek Revival to Craftsman. At its west end stands the Ulster House Hotel, formerly the Wellington, a large late-1800s Italianate frame hotel with a cupola and a wraparound porch, one of the few hotel buildings left from the peak of the Catskill resorts; Wikipedia called it vacant and under renovation.',
  'Walk Main Street from the Ulster House (pin, at Main and Academy streets) past the Methodist Episcopal church of about 1860, then north up Elm Street to the stone arch bridge, the Morton Memorial Library and the Shandaken Historical Museum in the old schoolhouse. Hotel interior not checked.',
  'A resort hamlet that kept its hotel and its stone bridges.',
  'Town streets, free to walk; the buildings are private. Park on Main Street.',
  links=[W('Pine Hill Historic District'),W('Ulster House Hotel')],brick='?'),
N('elm-street-stone-arch-bridge','Elm Street Stone Arch Bridge','Pine Hill','cat','landmark','standing',42.13333,-74.47944,'site','public','walk','','1897–1900s',
  'One of two small stone arch bridges the Shandaken Town Board approved for Pine Hill in 1897 and had built by the local mason Matthew G. Thompson. A 12-foot arch 6 feet above Alton Creek, of local stone with rough voussoirs and a keystone, it still carries Elm Street. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'The arch on the west side of the street, about 250 feet north of Main Street, with its blue and gold marker beside the creek; a modern culvert takes the creek out on the east side.',
  '',
  'A town street; look from the road edge.',
  links=[W('Elm Street Stone Arch Bridge'),HM(136223)],brick='no'),
N('mill-street-stone-arch-bridge','Mill Street Stone Arch Bridge','Pine Hill','cat','landmark','standing',42.13361,-74.48278,'site','public','walk','','1900',
  'The other of Pine Hill\'s two stone arch bridges: in 1900 the town hired the mason Matthew G. Thompson to build it for $208. A 12-foot arch 7 feet above the creek, with rough voussoirs, a central keystone and random coursed spandrel walls, still in use. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'The arch on Mill Street just south of where it splits from Bonnie View Avenue, about 800 feet west of the Elm Street bridge, in a wooded street of houses.',
  '',
  'A town street; look from the road edge and keep off the private yards.',
  links=[W('Mill Street Stone Arch Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('shandaken-historical-museum','District School No. 14 (Shandaken Historical Museum)','Pine Hill','cat','civic','standing',42.13472,-74.47917,'exact','open','walk','','mid-1920s–1960',
  'A schoolhouse built in the mid-1920s to replace an 1880s school that burned, mixing Colonial Revival and Craftsman styles, with a plan that followed the state\'s 1910 rules that ended the one-room school. Classes ended in 1960 when the small districts of western Ulster County were merged. After commercial uses it passed to the Town of Shandaken, and since 1989 it has been the town\'s historical museum. Listed on the National Register in 1997.',
  'The concrete-sided frame school on the east side of Academy Street, just south of Route 28. Hudson Valley One described displays from spinning wheels to school desks, and photographs and papers on the hamlets and their boarding houses.',
  '',
  'Town of Shandaken museum, Academy Street, Pine Hill. The museum\'s own hours could not be found; a 2018 Hudson Valley One article gave Fridays, Saturdays and Sundays from 10 a.m. Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('District School No. 14'),L('Hudson Valley One: Shandaken Museum in Pine Hill needs work (2018)','https://hudsonvalleyone.com/2018/04/10/shandaken-museum-in-pine-hill-needs-work')],
  brick='some',hours='?'),
]

